2. Verify logs can be seen in the middle of the screen and the created fault is visible in the logs
3. Verify physically that the e-stop button is released
4. Verify the system does not exit from fault mode
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-79
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWMC-125
TITLE: Fault Information 2
STATEMENT: >>>
An accepted PC clear-fault directive clears the main-control fault state, requests that the
HVPS clear its faults, and returns success without waiting for an HVPS response. A
clear-fault directive with a mismatched confirmation value initiates neither clear action.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
1. System is up and running without any issues
2. A PC command client can set directive confirmation values and record responses
3. A control-link monitor can capture commands sent from main control to the HVPS
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. When the system is running, push the e-stop button
2. Observe the active fault records and system state through the PC
3. Release the e-stop button
4. Monitor the HVPS control link while sending a correctly confirmed PC clear-fault directive
5. Record the PC directive response and subsequent fault update without supplying an HVPS response
6. Continue monitoring while sending a PC clear-fault directive with a mismatched confirmation value
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the system enters fault mode and publishes the e-stop fault
2. Verify the active fault record remains latched while the e-stop is active
3. Verify releasing the e-stop alone does not clear the fault
4. Verify main control sends one HVPS clear-fault command with zero-valued parameters
5. Verify the PC receives a successful directive response, followed by an empty active-fault
   update and a system state which is no longer faulted
6. Verify the PC receives an invalid response and main control sends no additional HVPS
   clear-fault command
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-78
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWMC-126
TITLE: Energized Fault Shutdown and Discharge
STATEMENT: >>>
When a fault is detected during an energized emission, verify that the firmware terminates
the emission, commands the energized outputs to zero, and remains in Fault Discharge until
the discharge feedback criteria are met.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
1. The system is ready to run an emission
2. A treatment plan is loaded and ready for emission
3. Instrumentation can record main-control state, HVPS commands and feedback, HVPS warming
   status, external timer state, emission indicators, coil outputs, and cabinet fan output
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Start the emission for the uploaded treatment plan
2. Confirm that emission is active and that kV and filament feedback are energized
3. Push the e-stop button
4. Record the commanded outputs and observable emission controls immediately after fault
   detection
5. Monitor the state, kV and filament feedback, HVPS warming status, and cabinet fan until
   the system enters Fault
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Verify the emission starts with its timer and emission indicator active
2. Verify the fault terminates emission, pauses the external timers, disables the emission
   indicators and high-voltage interlock, commands kV, filament, and grid to zero, and
   commands all three coil outputs to zero
3. Verify the system reports Fault Discharge and keeps the cabinet fan at high speed while
   kV feedback is at least 4 kV, filament feedback is above 700 mA, or HVPS warming status
   remains set
4. Verify the system enters Fault only after kV feedback is below 4 kV, filament feedback
   is at or below 700 mA, and HVPS warming status is clear
5. Verify Fault disables the emission and grid interlocks and retains all zero output
   commands
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-77
  ROLE: Verifies

[TEST_CASE]
UID: TC-H1FWMC-127
TITLE: Warmup Procedure
STATEMENT: >>>
Verify warmup is rejected safely when either emergency stop is open and starts only when
both inputs permit operation.
<<<
PREREQUISITES: >>>
1. The system is in COLD state without active faults
2. Command responses, state, heater command, coolant-pump output, and fault log are visible
3. The test fixture can pause state-machine event dispatch
<<<
STEPS: >>>
1. Open the base emergency stop and send a warmup request using a configured valid target
2. Restore the base input, open the remote emergency stop, and repeat the request
3. Restore both inputs, send the request while event dispatch is paused, open either
   emergency stop, and then resume dispatch
4. Restore both inputs and send the valid warmup request again
<<<
EXPECTED_BEHAVIOR: >>>
1. Each request made while an emergency stop is open returns ``SPR_ACCESS_ERROR``; the
   system remains COLD and does not change the heater or start the coolant pump
2. Opening an emergency stop after request acceptance but before state entry reports the
   ``Cannot warmup with e-stops open`` interlock fault and still leaves those outputs off
3. With both emergency stops released, the request succeeds, the system enters WARMUP,
   commands the requested target, and starts the coolant pump
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: RQ-H1FWMC-76
  ROLE: Verifies

